dc:title  a     owl:AnnotationProperty .

gomodel:6ab067da00015433
        a                      owl:Ontology ;
        rdfs:comment           "Curated with GO AI HUB. Mitochondrial lactate shortcut in mouse heart: Slc16a1 carries lactate across the inner mitochondrial membrane to matrix Ldhb, which oxidises it to pyruvate + NADH (PMID:42951956)." , "Curation notes: (1) MCT1 MF uses GO:0015129 (lactate transmembrane transporter activity) rather than the child GO:0015650 (lactate:proton symporter activity) because this paper does not test proton coupling at the IMM; GO:0015650 is already held by Slc16a1 via IBA/ISS. (2) MPC is an obligate MPC1/MPC2 heterodimer but has no ComplexPortal entry, so the pyruvate transport unit is enabled by Mpc1 alone. (3) MPC1 'occurs in' mitochondrial inner membrane is IDA from mitochondrial fractionation (Figs 1j, 2c); the IMM granularity leans on established prior knowledge. (4) Heart-failure, TAC and ischemia-reperfusion phenotypes are organism-level consequences and are deliberately not modelled as activities." , "Caveat on LDHA directionality: GO:0004459 is written as the reversible reaction, and direction here is expressed by has input (lactate) / has output (pyruvate). Note that LDHA kinetically favours the reverse direction (pyruvate -> lactate); PMID:42951956 itself notes the lower Km of LDHB for pyruvate favours forward lactate oxidation. The cytosolic LDH step is modelled with LDHA as the conventional-model enzyme (Fig 1a of that paper), but LDHB or a generic LDH may be the better enabler for net lactate oxidation." , "Built from PMID:42951956 (Cluntun et al., Nature 659:523-531, 2026). MCT1 (Slc16a1) imports lactate across the inner mitochondrial membrane; matrix LDHB oxidises it to pyruvate + NADH, feeding the ETC in parallel to the canonical MPC/pyruvate route. The mitochondrial lactate individual is shared between the MCT1 'has output' and the LDHB 'has input' (handoff), so 'provides input for' is inferred and not materialised." , "Cytosolic route added 2026-11-12: plasma-membrane MCT1 (Slc16a1) imports extracellular lactate into the cytosol; cytosolic LDHA oxidises it to pyruvate + NADH; that pyruvate is the same individual MPC1 imports into the matrix (handoff chain PM-MCT1 -> LDHA -> MPC1). IMPORTANT: these two units are NOT referenced to PMID:42951956. That paper reports the opposite for cardiomyocytes - cytosolic lactate uptake and pyruvate production were UNAFFECTED by loss of MCT1 (Fig 2d,e), and tissue lactate/pyruvate enrichment was unchanged, which is how the authors isolate the mitochondrial role from the plasma-membrane one. The PM unit is therefore evidenced by PMID:29026134 (MCT1 knockdown reduces plasma-membrane lactate flux, mouse adipocytes) and the LDHA unit by PMID:21041631 and PMID:17572440. Only the cardiomyocyte/heart context of the PM unit cites PMID:42951956." ;
        lego:conforms_to_gpad  false ;
        lego:modelstate        "development" ;
        dc:contributor         "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7299-6685" ;
        dc:date                "2026-11-16" ;
        dc:title               "In heart, Slc16a1 directly transports lactate into mitochondria for conversion to pyruvate by Ldhb, fueling the electron transport chain (Mouse)." ;
        pav:providedBy         "https://www.uniprot.org" , "http://geneontology.org" ;
        owl:versionIRI         gomodel:6ab067da00015433 ;
        biolink:in_taxon       obo:NCBITaxon_10090 .
